CAPI=2:

name: ::rc_top_core:1.0.0
description: >
  pcie_rc_top -- the Root Complex as ONE netlist, from the enumeration engine to
  the 32+4 PIPE seam. Three instantiations: pcie_enum_top (the engine),
  pcie_rq_rc_top (the Transaction Layer) and pcie_phy_top (Data Link Layer +
  LTSSM + logical PHY).

  This is a SEPARATE core from ::rc_core deliberately. rc_core is depended on by
  every RC bench, and pcie_rc_top is the first RC module that needs the PHY
  vertical; folding it into rc_core would make every one of those benches pull
  in ltssm, phy_receive and phy_transmit for a module they do not instantiate.
  Keeping it here means the dependency is paid only by the targets that actually
  build the full stack.

  pcie_rc_dl_top is NOT instantiated by pcie_rc_top -- it carries the second
  pcie_datalink_layer that this composition exists to drop -- but it stays in
  rc_core and keeps its own gate targets, and it remains the home of
  fc_init_sticky_r. pcie_rc_top consumes fc_initialized_o UNFILTERED.

  !! WHY pcie_phy_top.sv IS LISTED HERE RATHER THAN DEPENDED ON VIA phy_core.
  fusesoc:pcie:phy_core's rtl fileset carries example/pcie_top.v ALONGSIDE
  pcie_phy_top.sv, and that example instantiates pcie_phy_top without naming
  every pin. Depending on phy_core therefore drags a second, incomplete
  instantiation site into this build and turns D-FS.1's seven new outputs into
  seven PINMISSING warnings that have nothing to do with this module -- measured,
  not assumed: the first version of this file did depend on phy_core and produced
  exactly that. Listing the one file directly, on top of fusesoc:pcie:ltssm --
  which already carries the whole dependency set phy_core uses (packages, crc,
  bram, axis, base_uvm, dllp_core, lint, phy_receive, phy_transmit) -- builds
  exactly the netlist pcie_rc_top instantiates and nothing else.

  The example/ instantiations are left alone deliberately. There are FIVE of them
  and they are outside this rung's scope; updating them all to silence a warning
  would be a larger edit than the rung's own RTL, and example/ is not on any gate
  route reached from here.

  !! THIS CORE FILE LIVES AT THE REPOSITORY ROOT, not beside pcie_rc_top.sv, and
  that is deliberate: it names files in two directories (src/rc and
  src/pcie_phy_core), and FuseSoC deprecates a fileset path that escapes the core
  file's own directory. pcie_phy.core sits at the root for the same reason.

filesets:
  rtl:
    depend:
      - ::rc_core:1.0.0
      - fusesoc:pcie:ltssm:1.0.0
      - fusesoc:pcie:lint
    files:
      - src/pcie_phy_core/pcie_phy_top.sv
      - src/rc/pcie_rc_top.sv
    file_type: systemVerilogSource

targets:
  default:
    filesets:
      - rtl

  # Elaboration-only, for the integration site itself -- this is where a missing
  # pin shows up, with no bench in the way. Not a gate row; the gate row is
  # verilate_rc_top, which drives the stack.
  #
  # !! INVOKE WITH --build, NOT A BARE run:
  #       fusesoc run --build --target=lint ::rc_top_core
  # In lint-only mode there is no executable, so the sim flow's run stage raises
  # `TypeError: cannot unpack non-iterable NoneType object` AFTER a completely
  # successful lint. A bare `fusesoc run` therefore reports rc=1 on a clean
  # elaboration -- the inverse of the usual trap, and just as misleading.
  lint:
    flow: sim
    flow_options:
      tool: verilator
      mode: lint-only
      timescale: 1ns/1ps
      verilator_options:
        - --timing
        - waiver.vlt
    filesets: [rtl]
    toplevel: [pcie_rc_top]
